\f1\fs22 She had searched projects adjacent to Kebab, projects preceding Kebab, cancelled projects, projects whose budgets had migrated between departments, and projects that appeared to exist because a fastidious accountant had discovered there was more money in keeping their zombie corpses minimally animated. Eloise Shakti had interests in almost everything sufficiently expensive, dangerous or difficult to insure, but nothing among them had looked like another Gyges programme.\
That was before Hayley learned terms like 
\f2\i geometry
\f1\i0 , 
\f2\i coherence
\f1\i0 , and 
\f2\i remote resonance
\f1\i0  and even the ridiculous overwrought phrase, 
\f2\i multidimensionally colocated resonant concurrency event
\f1\i0 .\
For nothing more than the sake of investigative diligence, Hayley searched for those terms in the Ruined Castle intranet as well as the wider Shakti Enterprises data swamps. She didn\'92t expect to find anything, and gave her tablet a formal salute when she didn\'92t.\
Never mind. The juiciest evidence always hooked with inference and confirmation anyway. Hayley knew the heart of the case was skewered by working around the edges first. She looked up the address of the local not-for-profit municipal library and took a ground car to the centre of town at the top of the mountains.\
The library was even more not-for-profit than she\'92d feared, and was only accessible through the employee toilets of an all-night fish\'92n\'92chip shop. Hayley felt like she\'92d been through five slums and a tyre fire and by the time she\'92d found the public records room. Three computers squatted on a table with a wooden leg and a stuffed parrot. The other two legs were plastic. So was the parrot. The computers were broken. Hayley turned to leave and tripped on something hidden under a landslide of crumpled, oily wax-paper that smelled like fried potatoes and old tartare sauce.\
Hayley cleared the trash. And uncovered a computer. On the floor.\
It worked, which is to say, as soon as she jiggled its mouse, it offered her a free diabetes parasite report with the purchase of every pair of hearing aids (redeemable only in Nigeria). Hayley easily closed that window by pressing 
\f3 \uc0\u8984 \u8997 
\f1\fs20 TAB
\f4\fs22 \uc0\u9096 
\f1\fs20 CAPS 
\fs22 LOCK
\f5\fs20 \uc0\u9855 \u65039 
\f1  
\fs22 while typing \'93tax you\'94, and was searching the public data swamps inside eleven minutes and forty-seven seconds.\
It would have been twenty-three seconds faster if the smell of rancid tartare sauce hadn\'92t made her want to puke.\
Hayley typed all her new keywords into the search bar and dismissed the pop-up that warned her that accessing Fridge Force material could invalidate the warranty of her life. She was a veteran in the face of such threats by then.\
Results\'85\
Storage depots; a Shakti food subsidiary that made fake wasabi and chemical weapons; a ruling on valuing assets placed in stasis for tax purposes; a luxury hotel whose wine cellar had spent eleven minutes visiting the Jurassic period\'85\
One result scratched at Hayley\'92s recall: a Fridge Force Cease and Desist Order against an entity called Lysander Preservation Research. Where had she heard that name before? She pulled out her tablet and typed \'93Shakti / Lysander\'94 into the browser search bar. After all the ads for Nigerian hearing aids was an entry for a KinkedIn profile. Hayley clicked, and found herself reading a stale About page for Lysander Shakti\'97Eloise\'92s late husband and father of Alexander Shakti.\
\'93Poor bastard,\'94 Hayley remarked.\
There was no mention of Lysander Preservation Research in the profile, so Hayley went back to browser searching and quickly landed on a USIC corporate registration record.\
Lysander Preservation Research belonged to Lysander Holdings, which belonged to a medical technologies group, which belonged to an investment company whose principal activity appeared to be owning companies. A few registrations farther down, the trail reached Shakti Enterprises.\
Back on the tartare sauce floor computer, she clicked on the cease and desist order.\
The file was encrypted. A decryption plug-in offered to help for $1.37 per month for the next fourteen centuries. Fortunately, the first three minutes were free and Hayley could cancel any time after the next Day of the Dead.\
The notice ordered the cessation of an R&D programme to \'93develop safer closed resonance geometries in medical applications in times of armed conflict.\'94\
The phrasing 
\f2\i medical applications in times of armed conflict
\f1\i0  sounded to Hayley like typical Eloise word laundry.\
The notice included a programme number, names of research staff and references to the work Fridge Force had reviewed. Hayley followed one of them to a paper titled, \'93Enhanced closed resonance in multidimensionally colocated concurrency events\'94.\
The paper described configurations in which neighbouring fields could be allowed to interact without individual shielding. If Hayley was reading it correctly, there was a way to get around the Fridge Force beer coaster rule, \'93Two units in a building, you shield them. Two units in a room\'85 you don\'92t put two units in a room.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Let alone put three in a box and call it the Ark, 
\f1\i0 Hayley thought. What she was reading felt ominously familiar. The applications seemed civil: warehouses, hospitals, transport hubs. Hospitals especially, where there could be a machine that went 
\f2\i bing 
\f1\i0 under every bed and in every corner. These were places where individually shielding every unit cost money, occupied space, and offended accountants and gave private equity the hives.\
Apparently, Fridge Force had initially approved limited tests, then stopped them.\
Hayley opened a technical assessment.\
The first pages made resonant concurrency technology sound less interesting than flat-pack shelving: field strengths, containment distances, calibration tolerances. There was a table that seemed to regard punctuation as superfluous to requirements.\
\'93The word 
\f2\i stasis
\f1\i0  doesn\'92t appear anywhere in this stuff,\'94 Hayley. said to herself. She realised stasis was just one application of the theoretical research.\
Later tests showed that small changes in the phase relationship could move the strongest field response tens of metres from the generators, while other configurations concentrated a broad disturbance into a much smaller remote region. The research team had begun by trying to suppress interference; by the time Fridge Force intervened, it was learning how to shape and position it.\
Hayley opened a Fridge Force review. It discussed 
\f2\i directed field effects
\f1\i0 , 
\f2\i remote material disruption
\f1\i0  and 
\f2\i applications inconsistent with the declared purpose of the research
\f1\i0 .\
Hayley read the last phrase twice.\
Farther down, the assessment became less delicate.\

\f2\i The demonstrated effects have plausible military application.
\f1\i0 \
Eloise personally appealed the cessation order in a long, technically dense submission that she barbed with oblique and indirect threats. Investigation of a hazard is no evidence of an intention to exploit it, she wrote. Interacting fields needed to be studied to understand how they might interact badly and how they can be made safe. Electricity was dangerous, and it had been tamed, Eloise argued. These fields were no different. Closing the programme because its experiments measured the dangers, Eloise argued, was like banning hover-copt safety testing because the things handled like bricks with heated bucket seats.\
In another submission, Eloise attacked Fridge Force\'92s competence, methodology, jurisdiction and, in one footnote, its ability to stack toddler construction blocks.\
Eloise\'92s appeal failed, and the project was shuttered. Hayley found no evidence that it restarted under another name, moved elsewhere inside Shakti, or produced a weapon. She worked her way through project staff, facilities, equipment, the internal programme number, and found no flags.\
Hayley copied the cessation order, the technical review and Eloise\'92s appeal onto a thumb drive which required careful polishing with a wet-wipe once she pulled it from the floor computer.
